        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 245 and established contract principles (the doctrine of prevention), where a party's duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent, that party owes an implied duty of good faith and reasonable diligence to attempt to bring about the occurrence of the condition. If a party wrongfully prevents or hinders the occurrence of the condition (such as by failing to make any reasonable effort or application to obtain the required financing), the non-occurrence of the condition is EXCUSED by operation of law. Because the buyer made zero effort to apply for a loan, the buyer cannot rely on the failure of the financing condition as a defense to non-performance; the condition is excused, and the buyer is in breach (Option B). Option A is incorrect because express conditions are excused when the party relying on them wrongfully prevented their occurrence. Option C is incorrect because every contract contains an implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing that limits arbitrary discretion. Option D is incorrect because mortgage contingency clauses are standard and do not require monthly written renewal."
