        exp: "Rule: Under the traditional minority/common law retreat doctrine, an actor who is not at fault is NOT privileged to use deadly force in self-defense if the actor knows that he can avoid the necessity of using such force with complete safety by retreating. The only universal exception to the duty to retreat is the 'Castle Doctrine,' which applies when the actor is attacked inside his own dwelling house. Because the encounter occurred in a commercial warehouse (not Defendant's dwelling) and uncontroverted evidence established that Defendant knew of and had an open, completely safe avenue of retreat through an unlocked door five feet away, Defendant was legally obligated to retreat before employing lethal force. His failure to do so defeats justifiable self-defense (Option B). Option A is incorrect because it describes the majority 'stand your ground' rule, not the common law retreat rule. Option C is incorrect because the 'true man' doctrine is synonymous with 'stand your ground,' which retreat jurisdictions reject. Option D is incorrect because an unjustified intentional killing with a firearm is murder (or voluntary manslaughter under imperfect self-defense in applicable jurisdictions), not involuntary manslaughter."
